  "subject": "📱 Axios Finish Line: Smartphone revolt",
  "date": "Thu, 03 Sep 2026 21:29:22 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🏔️ Plus: Peak du jour | Thursday, September 03, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY PEPSICO \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Sep 03, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 286 words … 1 min. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Smartphone revolt \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Natalie Peeples/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Roughly half of American adults say they spend too much time on their phones — and more than half of younger adults are trying to kick the habit, according to Pew Research Center data out this week. \n\n Why it matters: Over the past two decades, smartphones have come to dominate daily life. Now Americans — especially younger adults — are pushing back against how much of their time and attention they command. 🧠 The big picture: Excessive smartphone use among kids and adults has come under growing scrutiny, with studies linking it to problems ranging from \" text neck \" to reduced cognitive performance to relationship trouble .\n\n For kids, the concerns have led schools to ban phones and governments to restrict social media . For adults, there's a growing market of products, apps and physical spaces designed to keep us off our devices. By the numbers: 53% of U.S. adults say they're on their phones too much. That share jumps to 70% when looking just at 18- to 29-year-olds.\n\n 45% of all adults say they've tried to cut back on phone time in the last year. When considering just those under 50, the share is 58%. 📰 The other side: People want time back from their phones, but they do acknowledge some perks of the devices, Pew found.\n\n 73% say smartphones help keep them informed, and 51% say they help them stay connected to others. Go deeper: Our tips for digital detox . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM PEPSICO\r\n\n \r\n AI can create farm resiliency \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n As farmers face more extreme weather, water stress and input cost uncertainty, PepsiCo sees AI as a tool for resilience.\n\n\r\n Looking ahead: When AI can identify smarter data, climate risk becomes more visible and efficiency more actionable for farmers.\n\n\r\n Read the Q&amp;A. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 📸 Pic to go! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Bob Schumacher North America's highest peak — designated Mount McKinley by the U.S. government and Denali by the state of Alaska — was captured by reader Bob Schumacher of Wheaton, Ill., this week.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
